WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.580
A retention policy turns a vague intention into an operational rule.

2
00:00:04.680 --> 00:00:15.480
For prompt history, the policy should state what is collected, why it is collected, how long each category is kept, who owns the decision, which systems are in scope, and what exceptions apply.

3
00:00:15.580 --> 00:00:24.220
It should cover visible conversations, hidden metadata, logs, exports, backups, evaluation records, and derived datasets.

4
00:00:24.500 --> 00:00:26.820
Retention should be tied to purpose.

5
00:00:26.920 --> 00:00:29.720
User continuity may justify one window.

6
00:00:29.820 --> 00:00:32.620
Debugging may justify a shorter technical window.

7
00:00:32.720 --> 00:00:41.600
Security monitoring, legal obligations, records rules, product quality review, billing, or compliance may justify different windows.

8
00:00:41.700 --> 00:00:47.940
When purposes differ, teams should avoid collapsing everything into the longest possible retention period.

9
00:00:48.040 --> 00:00:54.360
Separate categories make it easier to keep useful records without keeping unnecessary content forever.

10
00:00:54.640 --> 00:00:58.560
Indefinite retention because storage is cheap is a risky default.

11
00:00:58.660 --> 00:01:07.460
Stored prompt history can expand access risk, discovery obligations, privacy impact, breach exposure, and deletion complexity.

12
00:01:07.560 --> 00:01:19.880
A good policy asks whether the same goal can be met with less content, shorter storage, aggregation, redaction, sampling, or metadata instead of full prompt and response text.

13
00:01:20.160 --> 00:01:23.200
The policy also has to match real system behavior.

14
00:01:23.300 --> 00:01:36.980
Product settings, database jobs, log pipelines, analytics retention, observability tools, backup rotation, vendor settings, and manual exports should all align with the stated rule.

15
00:01:37.080 --> 00:01:43.240
A policy that cannot be implemented, tested, or audited will not protect users or the organization.

16
00:01:43.340 --> 00:01:55.500
The practical goal is a retention design that is documented, technically enforceable, reviewed regularly, and clear enough for engineering, security, privacy, legal, support, and product teams to follow.
